    <p class="video-cap">The Attic run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-hero-attic.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 16 Attic: a sloped plank ceiling with skylights above built-in shelves, a mint-green dresser and a desk with a typewriter, a hanging circular chair and a white birdcage on the floor" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>A finished attic rather than a junk store. The ceiling is planked and slopes down to both sides, broken by two skylights that throw hard light onto the floor. The back wall is a run of built-in shelves holding books, boxes, bottles and a red jug; a tall window stands at the right end of it. Along the right side sit a mint-green dresser with its top drawer pulled open, a stool, and a desk with a typewriter and a framed photograph on it. A circular hanging chair with a blue cushion hangs in the corner, and a metal-framed chair with a cushion reading <b>Love</b> sits beside the desk.</p>
    <p>The floor props are what the level actually runs on. A tall tripod floor lamp stands centre-left. A white birdcage sits on the boards with a small yellow bird inside. Crates of striped gourds are stacked near the shelves, with a red jug and rolled papers on top. It is a dense room, and the camera revisits the same corners several times in different light, which is why the source file has more usable frames per puzzle than either the Storage Room or the Garage.</p>
    <p>Structurally it is a mixed room: one genuine repair (the clock), one typed lock (the four-digit display), and two attribute boards (the octagon plate and the shape grid). The birdcage is the odd one out — it is not solved by entering anything, and it is the last thing you see before the results card.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Everything we could identify, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Red hexagonal clock</td><td>Shelf on the back wall</td><td>White face, gold bezel, brass pendulum; one hand position empty.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Clock hand</td><td>Inventory pickup</td><td>Labelled <b>Clock hand</b>; the matching repair for the wall clock.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Four-digit display</td><td>Wall-mounted lock</td><td>Up arrow above, down arrow below; reads <b>3549</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Octagon plate</td><td>Full-screen close-up</td><td>Square plate of small coloured octagons in orange, red, green, yellow and dark tones.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shape grid</td><td>Full-screen close-up</td><td>Five-by-five tiles, each holding a differently shaped, differently coloured piece.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>White birdcage</td><td>Floor, centre</td><td>Yellow bird inside; the last object before the results card.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mint-green dresser</td><td>Right wall</td><td>Top drawer pulled open; the drawer itself is lootable.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Desk and typewriter</td><td>Right wall, past the dresser</td><td>Typewriter and a framed photograph; the desk is a search surface.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Circular hanging chair</td><td>Corner, behind the desk</td><td>Wicker seat with a blue cushion.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Love chair</td><td>Metal frame, by the desk</td><td>Cushion printed with the word <b>Love</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tripod floor lamp</td><td>Floor, centre-left</td><td>Tall three-legged lamp; the room's main warm light before it brightens.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Skylights</td><td>Sloped ceiling, two</td><td>Hard daylight onto the boards; the room's only cool light.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Built-in shelves</td><td>Entire back wall</td><td>Books, boxes, bottles, a red jug, rolled papers on the top shelf.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gourd crates</td><td>Floor by the shelves</td><td>Stacked wooden crates of striped green gourds.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:00 · the title card</span>
      <strong>Level 16, Attic</strong>
      The level opens on its title over the full room: <b>Level 16 / Attic</b>. Behind the card you can already see the room's shape — planks sloping to both sides, skylights cutting light onto the floor, shelving across the back, and the white cage sitting in the middle of the boards. Read the ceiling first; it tells you which walls have shelves and which do not.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-level-title.jpg" alt="The Level 16 Attic title card in Rooms and Exits Chapter 3, over the attic with skylights, shelves and a birdcage" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=0s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:19 · the first pickup</span>
      <strong>Clock hand</strong>
      The first named item is the <b>Clock hand</b>, shown as a slim brass pointer lying against the inventory background, with the usual Use, Combine, Dismantle and Examine actions beneath it. The name is unusually literal for this game — it is not "old brass piece" or "metal fragment", it is the part, named. Whenever a Rooms &amp; Exits level names an item after the thing it fits, that item is a repair, not a key.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-clock-hand.jpg" alt="The Clock hand inventory item in the Attic, a slim brass pointer named Clock hand" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=19s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:19</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 1:39 · the clock</span>
      <strong>A red hexagon with one hand missing</strong>
      The clock sits on a shelf between a stack of books and a row of dark bottles: a red hexagonal case, a gold bezel, a white face with hour markers rather than numerals, and a short brass pendulum hanging under the case. On the face, one hand position appears occupied and the other appears empty — which is exactly what the Clock hand in your inventory is for. Fit the hand before you start hunting numbers; a working clock is the sort of thing this game uses to hand you a time.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-wall-clock.jpg" alt="The red hexagonal wall clock in the Attic on a shelf, with a white face, gold bezel, brass pendulum and a missing hand" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=99s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:39</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 2:59 · the inventory strip</span>
      <strong>Thirteen letters over a tile grid</strong>
      An inventory view, not a wall panel: the action buttons sit at the top and below them a strip of lettered tiles reads <b>R</b>, <b>N</b>, <b>G</b>, <b>N</b>, <b>D</b>, <b>P</b>, <b>A</b>, <b>O</b>, <b>B</b>, <b>E</b>, <b>C</b>, <b>K</b>, <b>L</b>. Because this is the player's own material rather than a lock, the ordering may be collection order rather than a solution. It is still worth writing down — thirteen letters is more than any single four-letter answer needs, so the extra letters are either decoys or belong to a second word.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-inventory-letters.jpg" alt="The Attic inventory showing a strip of lettered tiles reading R N G N D P A O B E C K L" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=179s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:59</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 3:59 · the room, roomy</span>
      <strong>Where everything actually sits</strong>
      The widest clean view of the level. Shelves run the full back wall with books, boxes, bottles and a red jug; the tall window stands at the right end. The mint-green dresser sits below with its top drawer hanging open, the stool in front of it, and the desk with the typewriter just past it. The hanging chair occupies the corner, and the birdcage sits alone on the boards with clear floor all around it — which is a hint in itself, because nothing else in this room has been given that much empty space.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-hero-attic.jpg" alt="A wide view of the Attic showing built-in shelves, a mint-green dresser with an open drawer, a desk with a typewriter and a white birdcage alone on the floor" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=239s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:59</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 4:39 · the number lock</span>
      <strong>3 5 4 9</strong>
      A four-digit display on a metal face, with a small up arrow set above the digits and a down arrow below them — the arrows cycle the value rather than adding a row of keys. Read left to right, twice, from the whole frame and from a crop: <b>3</b>, <b>5</b>, <b>4</b>, <b>9</b>. Both passes agreed on all four digits. This is the only typed number in the Attic.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-code-dial.jpg" alt="The Attic four-digit number lock showing 3549 with an up arrow above the digits and a down arrow below" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=279s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:39</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 4:59 · the octagon plate</span>
      <strong>Colour is the variable, not number</strong>
      A square plate carrying a tight grid of small octagons in orange, red, green, yellow and dark tones, arranged in a repeating pattern with a few pieces clearly out of place. There is no text on the plate and no dial around it, so it belongs to the same family as the shape grid later in the run: you are matching an arrangement of attributes, not entering a code. Note which colours repeat and which are singletons before you start moving anything.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-octagon-plate.jpg" alt="The Attic octagon plate puzzle, a square board of small orange, red, green and yellow octagons in a repeating pattern" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=299s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:59</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 5:59 · the shape grid</span>
      <strong>Five by five, all different</strong>
      A five-by-five grid of tiles, each holding one flat shape in one colour. What makes it a puzzle rather than a pattern is that the shapes vary across the board instead of repeating a single motif — the grid is asking you to place or match distinct pieces. No frame in the sampled run shows it resolved, so this page describes the board's layout and stops there rather than inventing a sequence.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-shape-grid.jpg" alt="The Attic five by five shape grid with each tile holding a differently shaped and coloured piece" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=359s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:59</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 8:23 · the birdcage</span>
      <strong>The room lights up and the cage is what you see</strong>
      Late in the run the attic brightens: the lamp glare is gone from the corner, the planks lose their shadow, and the white birdcage stands out on the floor with the yellow bird inside it. The camera settles on the cage and holds. In a level that has spent eight minutes handing you clocks, plates and grids, ending on an object you never entered a number into is deliberate — it is the room's last beat, not a puzzle you missed.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-attic-lit.jpg" alt="The Attic lit up late in the level, with the white birdcage and its yellow bird standing on the floor" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=503s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">8:23</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 8:55 · the ending</span>
      <strong>+50 coins, Time 8:43, piggy full</strong>
      The results card is complete: <b>LEVEL COMPLETED!</b>, a coin reward of <b>+50</b>, <b>eight of ten</b> efficiency segments filled, <b>Time: 8:43</b> in orange, and a piggy-bank line reading <b>300</b> with the bank flagged <b>Full!</b>. All three figures were read twice from separate passes and agreed both times. At 8:43 the Attic is the slowest of the three utility rooms and still pays the same +50 as the Storage Room's 6:50 — the reward scale here is flat, not time-based.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l16-level-complete.jpg" alt="The Level 16 Attic results card in Rooms and Exits showing Level Completed, a +50 coin reward, an efficiency bar eight of ten segments filled, Time 8:43 and a 300 coin piggy bank reward" width="1200" height="527" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Ww2frnjLNw&t=535s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">8:55</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>You treat the Clock hand as a key.</b> It is a repair part, and the red hexagonal clock on the shelf is missing exactly that. If the clock is still showing one hand, you have not fitted it yet — and a room that hands you a time is a room that expects you to make the clock work first.</p>
    <p><b>You read the letter strip as an answer.</b> Thirteen letters is longer than any lock in this room. That strip is an inventory view — the action buttons are above it — so it is showing what you collected, not what you must enter. Copy it down, but do not try to type it anywhere.</p>
    <p><b>You keep pressing the arrows on the four-digit lock one digit at a time.</b> The arrows cycle the value, so holding or repeating moves faster than tapping. The digits to reach are <b>3 5 4 9</b>; if you are stuck on the third digit, the answer is <b>4</b>, not <b>5</b>, which is the mistake the double read was there to catch.</p>
    <p><b>You try to solve the birdcage.</b> Nothing in the recording enters anything into the cage, and it is the last object on screen before the results card. If you are standing in front of it looking for an interaction, you are probably already done.</p>
    <p><b>You hunt for a second number pad.</b> The Attic has one typed lock. Everything else — the octagon plate, the five-by-five grid — is attribute matching. Counting pieces and colours is the right instinct; looking for digits is not.</p>

    <h2>Where the Attic sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it lists twenty-four rooms. Attic is room <b>16 of 24</b>, straight after the Garage and immediately before the Terrace. It closes the chapter's run of three utility spaces — cellar, garage, attic — and it is the only one of the three that ends with a fully readable results card and a mid-length run.</p>
    <p>Mechanically it is a step up in variety. The Storage Room was colours and bars, the Garage was colours and shapes, and the Attic combines a physical repair with a typed lock and two attribute boards. If you are working through Chapter 3 in order, this is the room where the game stops teaching one mechanic and starts mixing them.</p>

