    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. What 3549 unlocks.</b> The digits are solid — two independent passes, whole frame and crop, agreed on all four. What no frame shows is the lock accepting them: the camera reads the display and then cuts. Treat <b>3549</b> as the state on the lock at 4:39, and as the value to try if your own lock starts blank.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The order of the thirteen letters.</b> The strip is legible as a set — R, N, G, N, D, P, A, O, B, E, C, K, L — but because it is an inventory view rather than a wall panel, the left-to-right order may be collection order. This page prints the letters and does not present them as a sequence, and it does not manufacture a word from them.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. How the octagon plate and the shape grid resolve.</b> Both boards are described from clean close-ups, including their colour families and the fact that the shape grid's tiles differ from one another. Neither board is shown in a solved state anywhere in the recording, so no arrangement or sequence is printed for them.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. The clock's final time.</b> The wall clock is clearly short one hand and the Clock hand is clearly in the inventory, but the recording never holds on the face long enough after the fitting to read a settled time. If the room is asking you to read a clock, that value is the one thing this page cannot give you.
    </div>
